The '''Isuzu Aska''' was a nameplate used by [[Isuzu]] of Japan to denote its [[midsize car|mid-size]] [[sedan (car)|sedan]]s from 1983 to 2002. Originally the Aska was a version of [[General Motors]]' [[GM J platform|J-car]] produced by Isuzu, but after Isuzu pulled out of manufacturing passenger cars the nameplate was applied to [[badge engineering|rebadged]] versions of the [[Subaru Legacy]] (1990 to 1993) and [[Honda Accord]] (1994 to 2002) sold through Isuzu's Japanese distribution network.

The Aska replaced the [[Isuzu Florian]] in Isuzu's lineup and was discontinued in 2002 without a replacement.

The name comes from the [[Japanese language|Japanese]] word, "Asuka", which is the old name of the [[Asuka, Nara|Asuka Village]] in the [[Nara Prefecture]] of Japan. Because the name "Asuka" is likely to be mispronounced in foreign countries, the "u" was taken away from the name, presenting the model as the "Aska".
