===Export markets===
===<small>South America (Chile, Ecuador)</small>===
From 1984, the Aska was assembled in [[Arica]], [[Chile]] from [[Complete knock down|CKD]] kits and sold as '''Chevrolet Aska''' in the Chilean domestic market and in [[Ecuador]]. Chilean Askas came in three equipment levels (LT, Limited, and Deluxe), with two engines and either automatic or five-speed manual transmissions. While the two lesser versions both received a {{cvt|91|PS|kW|0}} 1.8-litre coupled to the five-speed, the Deluxe got the larger 2-litre with {{cvt|100|PS|kW|0}}, only fitted with the automatic transmission.<ref>{{citation | title = Chevrolet Aska ''(advertisement)'' | url = https://www.flickr.com/photos/ifhp97/4588034361/sizes/o | year = 1986 | publisher = GM Automotriz Chile (GMAC) }}</ref>

===<small>South-East Asia and New Zealand</small>===
The Aska was exported to [[Southeast Asia]], where it was known as the '''Isuzu JJ'''.<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=vjMcAQAAMAAJ&q=%22holden+camira%22+%22isuzu+aska%22 ''Singapore Business''], Volume 8, Issues 1-6, Singapore Press Holdings, 1984, page 108</ref> It was also exported to [[New Zealand]] as the [[Holden Camira]] (JJ) between 1984 and 1987.<ref>[https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/CHP19860410.2.129.3 Camira SLE one of better handling Japanese cars], ''[[The Press]]'', 10 April 1986, page 22</ref> This replaced the Australian-sourced JD Camira, because its predecessor, the JB Camira, had fared badly in the New Zealand market.<ref>[https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/CHP19850117.2.76.1 New Camira put through paces], ''[[The Press]]'', 17 January 1985, page 10</ref> However, the JD series wagon was retained, albeit imported concurrently from Australia rather than locally assembled.<ref>[https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/newspapers/CHP19850919.2.205.1 An honest-to-goodness waggon], ''[[The Press]]'', 19 September 1985, page 40</ref> [[Holden New Zealand|General Motors New Zealand]] switched back to the updated Australian version (JE) in 1987 due to the strengthening of Japanese Yen. In [[Indonesia]], the two-liter version with {{convert|74|kW|PS|abbr=on}} was briefly sold as the "Holden Aska" alongside the 1.6-liter Camira.<ref>{{cite magazine | url = https://books.google.com/books?id=0BtPAQAAIAAJ&q=%22Holden+Aska%22 | title = Kebakaran di gudang PT Udatin Panjung Perak | trans-title = Fire at PT Udatin Panjung Perak warehouse | language = id | magazine = Tempo | volume = 15 | issue = 31 | publisher = Badan Usaha Jaya Press | location = Japansan Jaya Raya | date = 25 September 1985 | page = 59 }}</ref>

