==Second generation (1990&ndash;1993)==
{{Infobox automobile
| image        = Isuzu aska bcl 2.0cxtypeg 1 f.jpg
| name         = Second generation
| production   = 1990&ndash;1993
| assembly     = Japan: [[Ōta, Gunma]] (Subaru)
| engine       = 1.8&nbsp;L ''[[Subaru EJ engine#EJ18|EJ18]]'' [[Flat-four engine|H4]]<br>2.0&nbsp;L ''[[Subaru EJ engine#EJ20|EJ20]]'' H4
| transmission = 5-speed [[manual transmission|manual]]<br>4-speed [[automatic transmission|automatic]]
| layout       = [[FF layout|FF]] (standard)<br>[[All-wheel drive|AWD]] (optional on 2.0L)
| aka          = [[Subaru Legacy (first generation)|Subaru Legacy]]<br>Isuzu Aska CX<br>Isuzu Aska E/T/V
}}
With the exception of the American market, the J platform did not continue into the 1990s. Not having the resources to develop a mid-size car on their own, Isuzu forged a short-lived alliance with [[Subaru]]. This provided for Isuzu to supply Subaru with [[Isuzu Bighorn]]s (rebadged as the Subaru Bighorn), while Isuzu rebadged Subaru's mid-size [[Subaru Legacy|Legacy]] sedan as the Isuzu Aska, beginning in June 1990. Both rebadged models were sold only in the Japanese market.<ref>{{cite web|title=Isuzu Aska Second Generation 1990–1993|periodical=Gaukmotors.co.uk|publisher=|url=http://gaukmotors.co.uk/motorpedia/car-make/isuzu/aska-second-generation-19901993|url-status=|format=|access-date=2022-10-31|archive-url=|archive-date=|last=|date=|year=|language=en|pages=|quote=}}</ref> There was also a [[Subaru Leone#Third generation|Subaru Leone]] Van version briefly available, badged as the '''Isuzu Geminett II'''.

The [[Subaru EJ engine|EJ series]] engine was offered in both 1.8 and 2.0&nbsp;liter [[Engine displacement|displacement]]s. The engine has either single or double [[overhead camshaft]] architecture and [[pent-roof combustion chamber|pent-roof]], [[Crossflow cylinder head|cross flow]] cylinder firing chambers.
 
The 2.0 liter fuel delivery was managed with [[Fuel injection#Multi-point injection|sequential multiport fuel injection]], and the 1.8 liter engine used a modified fuel delivery system with single point throttle body fuel injection called [[Fuel injection#SPI|SPFI]]. The DOHC 2.0 liter [[Naturally-aspirated engine|non turbocharged]] engine had a dual stage [[Inlet manifold|intake manifold]].

The 2.0 liter vehicle came optional with Subaru's AWD system while the 1.8 came with FWD only. As with all other Askas, this was only ever available with four-door saloon bodywork. The Aska was discontinued in May 1993, as the Subaru deal came to an end.

